      <title>After Days of Digital Outages, Americans Wonder if the Country Is Breaking</title>
      <link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/us/politics/outages-flights-banks-tech.html</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/us/politics/outages-flights-banks-tech.html</guid>
      <pubDate>Wed, 18 Nov 2026 01:36:41 +0000</pubDate>
      <enclosure url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/18/multimedia/18nat-breakdown-hmvc/18nat-breakdown-hmvc-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" length="0" type="image/jpeg" />
      <media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/18/multimedia/18nat-breakdown-hmvc/18nat-breakdown-hmvc-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" medium="image" />
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/18/multimedia/18nat-breakdown-hmvc/18nat-breakdown-hmvc-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;margin-bottom:10px;" alt="After Days of Digital Outages, Americans Wonder if the Country Is Breaking" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The digital outages that began on Thursday may not be linked, but they have left Americans with a sense that the nation is off-kilter, with no one ready to fix it.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
